    coverageRequired: "South Dakota does not set a minimum employee-count threshold for private employers. Under SDCL 62-3-3, every employer and employee is presumed to have accepted the Workers' Compensation title unless an exception applies (SDCL 62-3-4 to 62-3-5.1). 'Employee' under SDCL 62-1-3 excludes any person whose work is not in the usual course of the employer's trade or business. No employer-size safe harbor was found in the statutes reviewed; treat South Dakota as requiring coverage once an employer has one or more common-law employees, subject to the independent-contractor and casual-labor exclusions in SDCL 62-1-3.",
    soleProprietor: "Not automatically an 'employee' under SDCL 62-1-3 (the statute covers a person 'in the services of another'), so a sole proprietor is not required to carry coverage on themselves. SDCL 62-1-3 does allow 'an employer performing labor incidental to the employer's occupation' to purchase workers' compensation insurance and be deemed an employee for coverage purposes -- i.e., election is by purchasing a policy that includes the owner, not by a separate state filing.",
    partners: "Same treatment as sole proprietors under SDCL 62-1-3 (excluded by default from the 'employee' definition; may elect coverage by purchasing/endorsing a policy to include the partner). No state-filed exemption or election certificate was located.",
