    corporateOfficers: "Corporate officers are covered ('employees') by default. SDCL 62-3-5.1 lets an 'executive officer of a corporation' reject coverage by serving written notice -- personally or by certified mail -- on the corporation itself, either at the time of the officer's election/appointment or more than 30 days before an injury, death, or (for occupational disease under ch. 62-8) 30 days before contracting the disease. The rejection can later be withdrawn the same way. This is a private notice to the corporation, not a filing with any state agency, and the statute text located does not impose a cap on the number of officers who may reject coverage (a secondary source claimed a cap exists but this could not be confirmed in the statute itself -- treat that claim as unverified).",
    construction: null,
    howToFile: "There is no state-run exemption certificate or registry in South Dakota (unlike Tennessee). Sole proprietors/partners obtain coverage for themselves affirmatively through their insurance policy if they want it (no filing to go without it). Corporate officers who want to be excluded reject coverage by written notice served on their own corporation per SDCL 62-3-5.1 -- no state agency, form, or fee was identified for this process.",
    agencyName: "South Dakota Department of Labor and Regulation, Division of Labor and Management -- Workers' Compensation",
