    coverageRequired: "Mandatory once an employer 'regularly employs more than two part-time or full-time employees' (i.e., 3 or more). Subcontractors' employees count toward a general contractor's total under the 'statutory employer' doctrine (Va. Code 65.2-302) when the subcontractor is performing the same trade/business/occupation as the contractor or fulfilling a contract obligation of the contractor -- so a GC can be required to carry coverage based on combined headcount even if the GC itself has 2 or fewer direct employees. Executive officers count as employees for purposes of the headcount. Underground coal mine operators cannot use the small-employer exemption regardless of count. Domestic/household workers employed for care/comfort/convenience of household members are excluded with no numerical limit.",
    soleProprietor: "A sole proprietor with no employees and no subcontractors is not required to carry coverage. Virginia does NOT issue or accept any waiver/exemption/opt-out filing for a business that is not required to carry coverage in the first place -- there is simply no filing needed. Voluntary coverage can be purchased if desired.",
    partners: "Not separately addressed in the sources reviewed beyond the general employee-count and statutory-employer rules; partners performing work are generally treated like any other person providing services for pay when assessing whether the business exceeds the 2-employee threshold. Not verified beyond this -- confirm specifics with the Commission if partnership-specific treatment matters.",
    llcMembers: "Single-member LLCs: the member is treated as an employee. For multi-member LLCs the statute is silent; treatment depends on whether the member performs work and is compensated as an employee, separate from ownership/membership duties.",
    corporateOfficers: "Corporate officers and LLC managers are, by default, defined as employees and are NOT exempt from coverage (i.e., they count toward the employer's headcount and must be covered like any other employee). The only exception: non-compensated officers of a 501(c)(3) tax-exempt corporation or of a property owners' association may be exempt. Officers and LLC managers are the only categories of employee who may affirmatively REJECT coverage -- they do so by filing a Rejection of Coverage form (Form 16A) with the Virginia Workers' Compensation Commission and with the insurer, but only after the business already has a valid workers' comp policy in place (you cannot reject coverage that never existed). No cap on the number of officers who may file a rejection was found in the sources reviewed.",
    construction: "General contractors are 'statutory employers' under Va. Code 65.2-302 and are liable for workers' comp coverage of subcontractors' employees performing the contractor's trade/business or fulfilling its contract obligations. Subcontractor employees are added to the contractor's own employee count when determining whether the more-than-two-employee threshold is met, regardless of whether the subcontractor separately carries its own coverage. Contractors should keep proof of subcontractors' coverage.",
